# Day 33 — the organizer on a cron schedule.
#
# Install with `crontab docker/crontab.example` after replacing the paths and
# the image tag, or copy the line below into an existing crontab. Everything on
# this line exists to make one nightly run of a one-shot container behave like a
# scheduled job rather than like a person at a keyboard:
#
#   flock -n      a second run cannot start while the first is still moving
#                 files. Cron does not know how long a run takes, so overlap is
#                 a matter of when, not if.
#   --rm          a scheduled job must not leave a container per night behind.
#   --network none  the organizer reads and moves local files; it has no reason
#                 to reach a network, so the container is not given one.
#   --read-only + --tmpfs /tmp
#                 only the two mounted volumes are writable. A bug that writes
#                 into the image's filesystem fails here instead of on the host.
#   -v /srv/inbox:/data:rw
#                 an absolute host path, mounted at the directory the image's
#                 WORKDIR points at. Relative mounts resolve against cron's own
#                 working directory, which is not yours.
#   -v /srv/datheon-state:/state:rw
#                 the rotating log (DATHEON_LOG_DIR=/state/logs) and any --log
#                 output, kept across runs because the container is not.
#   >> ... 2>&1  cron mails whatever a job writes to stdout/stderr. Without the
#                 redirect a nightly run leaves no history but the mail spool.
#   --dry-run     in the example on purpose: an example that moves files the
#                 first time it is installed is a trap. Remove it once the log
#                 shows the plan you expected.
#
# The image is tagged with the release version, never `latest`: a scheduled job
# whose behaviour changes without a commit on this host is not a schedule, it is
# a surprise.
#
# The job is one physical line because cron has no line continuation — a
# backslash at the end of a crontab entry is not joined, it is part of the
# command. Wrap it in your editor if you must, not in the file you install.

SHELL=/bin/sh
PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin

# m   h   dom mon dow   command
  15   2   *   *   *   flock -n /var/lock/datheon-organizer.lock docker run --rm --network none --read-only --tmpfs /tmp -v /srv/inbox:/data:rw -v /srv/datheon-state:/state:rw datheon-organizer:1.1.0 --dry-run >> /var/log/datheon-organizer.log 2>&1
